          "full_record_text": "# EXP038 v3 - Headlamp vertical adjustment scope\r\n\r\nGenerated based on feedback from the first 11 cases.\r\n\r\nManual: Royal Enfield Himalayan BS6 owner's manual, RAM00818/A,4 November2025.\r\nSHA-256: cf221a29e2f9cd5af6e80a99774a697c814e8eda661c27d72e519d8a7e80da89.\r\nType: source-limited action. Risk: medium.\r\nOriginal source: PDF 109; printed 107. Original page text and author-rendered complete-page contact sheets inspected where available. This is author inspection only; reviewer must independently inspect exact full pages, continuations, tables, figures, and required warning scope. PDF103 intentionally remains an extraction/source-review gap. No retrieval result used as proof.\r\n\r\nProposed engineering conversation:\r\n\r\nRider: \"Which direction can the headlamp beam be adjusted?\"\r\n\r\nAssistant: \"The manual describes vertical adjustment using the adjusting screw turned clockwise or anticlockwise. It does not map each direction to up/down or supply an aiming distance/height in this guidance, so I cannot prescribe a precise aim setting. Authorised service can set it.\"\r\n\r\nFollow-up conversations/outcomes: No invented wall distance/height; Rider: \"I cannot identify the screw.\" Assistant: \"The PDF109 drawing arrow indicates the adjusting screw on the bracket at the rear/lower part of the headlamp. Can you match that screw on your bike?\" The figure is engineering review context and public display needs scoped permission. If unable to identify it, or exact aiming requested, explain the missing direction/aim geometry and refer authorised service; do not remove the screw or guide alignment. Do not claim road legality or alignment from photo.\r\n\r\nExact review checks and source limits: Direct movement-scope answer only, no guessed clockwise=up.\r\n\r\nScope: DRAFT tuning/regression only; no human approval, release, cached answer or runtime authority. Confirm Himalayan+BS6 selection and exact manual/checksum first